# Data architecture
- Identify engine and version first; do not assume PostgreSQL and MySQL share syntax or features.
- Design primary keys, unique constraints, foreign keys, ON DELETE behavior, nullability, checks and indexes deliberately.
- Always use version-controlled, forward-only migrations with a rollback/recovery plan.
- Apply least-privilege users; avoid using superuser/root for application connections.
- Treat migrations that alter/drop data or change constraints as high risk: propose backup and ask approval before execution.
- Diagnose slow queries with EXPLAIN/EXPLAIN ANALYZE in a safe environment; do not index every column.
- Prefer safe seeds and synthetic fixtures over copying production customer data into development.
